<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>

<overview>
<p>
An AI model's system prompt (also called a developer prompt) defines the instructions
that govern the model's behavior. When untrusted, user-controlled input is used to build
the system prompt, an attacker can override those instructions, bypass safety controls,
leak sensitive data, or cause the model to perform unintended operations. This is known
as prompt injection.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Keep untrusted input out of the system prompt. Place user-controlled data only in
user-role messages, and rely on the system prompt (which should be composed solely of
trusted, application-defined text) to constrain how that data is handled. Where user
input must influence the system prompt, validate and sanitize it against a strict
allow-list first.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
In the following example, a value taken from an HTTP request is used directly as the
OpenAI system prompt, which allows an attacker to control the model's instructions.
</p>
<sample src="SystemPromptInjectionBad.cs" />
<p>
In the fixed version, the untrusted value is placed in a user-role message instead, and
the system prompt is a fixed, trusted string.
</p>
<sample src="SystemPromptInjectionGood.cs" />
</example>

<references>
<li>OWASP: <a href="https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/">LLM01:2025 Prompt Injection</a>.</li>
<li>MITRE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1427.html">CWE-1427: Improper Neutralization of Input Used for LLM Prompting</a>.</li>
</references>

</qhelp>
